rites through any member available from the other members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster; node2 and node3 joined via SST (rsync), confirmed by journal logs. wsrep_cluster_size=3, wsrep_cluster_status=Primary, wsrep_ready=ON, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced on node1 (cmd-dbd8c9c2e84e4c96). A row inserted on node2 (cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b) was read back identically on node1 (cmd-260ee4704be343f4) and node3 (cmd-a7a83ba8c971435f), directly demonstrating multi-master write propagation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e6f3a3222fbf4ac0\",\"cmd-eebb6b2282d0418e\",\"cmd-0b32f068b5234f91\",\"cmd-dbd8c9c2e84e4c96\",\"cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b\",\"cmd-260ee4704be343f4\",\"cmd-a7a83ba8c971435f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3's mariadb was stopped (cmd-43a9b41726414d2a, is-active returned inactive). While node3 was down, node1 showed wsrep_cluster_size=2, wsrep_cluster_status=Primary and successfully accepted a new insert (cmd-8941c1909df14d91); node2 confirmed seeing that new row (cmd-d8914df87250406a), directly demonstrating a bounded disruption with continued availability.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-43a9b41726414d2a\",\"cmd-8941c1909df14d91\",\"cmd-d8914df87250406a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Resynchronize the unavailable member automatically when it returns\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3's mariadb was restarted (cmd-2bc86877fa1c403c) and, without manual intervention beyond systemctl start, rejoined at wsrep_cluster_size=3 with wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced and contained the row written while it was offline (cmd-fde264e57c254058), directly showing automatic IST resynchronization and data recovery.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2bc86877fa1c403c\",\"cmd-fde264e57c254058\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"State-changing executor commands were all purposeful: package installation, firewall port openings limited to the required Galera/MariaDB ports (3306, 4567/tcp+udp, 4568, 4444), /etc/hosts population from CLUSTER_HOSTS, galera.cnf authoring, cluster bootstrap/join, the bounded stop/start of node3 for the availability test (fully restored), and the mandatory one-at-a-time reboot protocol on all three nodes with pre/post boot_id and cluster-health verification. No unnecessary mutations, failed services, or unsafe exposure were introduced; after-executor snapshots show zero failed services and only expected new listeners (mariadbd on 3306/4567) on all nodes. One minor residue: the galeratest database/table created for write-propagation testing (cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b) was not dropped afterward, leaving small test data as permanent state — low severity since it is inert and does not conflict with the cluster's function.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3a17e3bbf3044921\",\"cmd-27a4687a5a404c3f\",\"cmd-37cb301e6f2b4c7c\",\"cmd-3c6eb870e4414116\",\"cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes from the task contract — cluster formation with cross-member write visibility, continued availability during a bounded single-node outage, and automatic resynchronization on recovery — are directly and coherently demonstrated by observed command output rather than executor claims alone. The independent mandatory node-restart protocol was also completed cleanly for all three nodes, with matching boot_id changes and full cluster/data recovery (size 3, Primary, Synced) confirmed by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good; the only flaw is leftover test data (galeratest.t) that was never cleaned up, a minor and low-risk residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"The reboot-in-progress window itself (between the backgrounded reboot ack and the node becoming reachable again) was not directly observed with cluster-state commands, since the node was unreachable during that interval by design; only the pre- and post-reboot healthy states were captured, which is expected for this type of transition.\",\"No load-balancer or external client connectivity test was performed; write/read verification was done via direct mysql client connections on each node rather than through a proxy layer, though the task did not require one.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster; writes through any member available from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster; node2 and node3 joined via SST (rsync), confirmed by journal logs. wsrep_cluster_size=3, wsrep_cluster_status=Primary, wsrep_ready=ON, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced on node1 (cmd-dbd8c9c2e84e4c96). A row inserted on node2 (cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b) was read back identically on node1 (cmd-260ee4704be343f4) and node3 (cmd-a7a83ba8c971435f), directly demonstrating multi-master write propagation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6f3a3222fbf4ac0","cmd-eebb6b2282d0418e","cmd-0b32f068b5234f91","cmd-dbd8c9c2e84e4c96","cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b","cmd-260ee4704be343f4","cmd-a7a83ba8c971435f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's mariadb was stopped (cmd-43a9b41726414d2a, is-active returned inactive). While node3 was down, node1 showed wsrep_cluster_size=2, wsrep_cluster_status=Primary and successfully accepted a new insert (cmd-8941c1909df14d91); node2 confirmed seeing that new row (cmd-d8914df87250406a), directly demonstrating a bounded disruption with continued availability.","evidence_ids":["cmd-43a9b41726414d2a","cmd-8941c1909df14d91","cmd-d8914df87250406a"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Resynchronize the unavailable member automatically when it returns","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's mariadb was restarted (cmd-2bc86877fa1c403c) and, without manual intervention beyond systemctl start, rejoined at wsrep_cluster_size=3 with wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced and contained the row written while it was offline (cmd-fde264e57c254058), directly showing automatic IST resynchronization and data recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2bc86877fa1c403c","cmd-fde264e57c254058"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"State-changing executor commands were all purposeful: package installation, firewall port openings limited to the required Galera/MariaDB ports (3306, 4567/tcp+udp, 4568, 4444), /etc/hosts population from CLUSTER_HOSTS, galera.cnf authoring, cluster bootstrap/join, the bounded stop/start of node3 for the availability test (fully restored), and the mandatory one-at-a-time reboot protocol on all three nodes with pre/post boot_id and cluster-health verification. No unnecessary mutations, failed services, or unsafe exposure were introduced; after-executor snapshots show zero failed services and only expected new listeners (mariadbd on 3306/4567) on all nodes. One minor residue: the galeratest database/table created for write-propagation testing (cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b) was not dropped afterward, leaving small test data as permanent state — low severity since it is inert and does not conflict with the cluster's function.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a17e3bbf3044921","cmd-27a4687a5a404c3f","cmd-37cb301e6f2b4c7c","cmd-3c6eb870e4414116","cmd-e3a2c6daf15c4a8b","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes from the task contract — cluster formation with cross-member write visibility, continued availability during a bounded single-node outage, and automatic resynchronization on recovery — are directly and coherently demonstrated by observed command output rather than executor claims alone. The independent mandatory node-restart protocol was also completed cleanly for all three nodes, with matching boot_id changes and full cluster/data recovery (size 3, Primary, Synced) confirmed by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good; the only flaw is leftover test data (galeratest.t) that was never cleaned up, a minor and low-risk residue.","limitations":["The reboot-in-progress window itself (between the backgrounded reboot ack and the node becoming reachable again) was not directly observed with cluster-state commands, since the node was unreachable during that interval by design; only the pre- and post-reboot healthy states were captured, which is expected for this type of transition.","No load-balancer or external client connectivity test was performed; write/read verification was done via direct mysql client connections on each node rather than through a proxy layer, though the task did not require one."]},"ttft_ms":7509,"type":"result","duration_ms":47012,"uuid":"c8494c79-defb-4c54-893e-ebab22e05d26","ttft_stream_ms":1053,"time_to_request_ms":37,"first_content_frame_ms":1489,"queued_turn_count":0}
